<section class="page-hero">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/blog/">&larr; Back to Blog</a></div>
    <div class="section-eyebrow">2 June 2026</div>
    <h1>How Often Does a School Need a Fire Risk Assessment?</h1>
  </div>
</section>
<section>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="prose" style="max-width:720px">
<p>It's one of the most common questions we're asked by site managers and business managers: how often does our fire risk assessment actually need to be redone? The honest answer is that there's no fixed statutory number written into law &mdash; but that doesn't mean "whenever we get round to it" is an acceptable answer either.</p>
<h2>What the law actually says</h2>
<p>Under the Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005, the "responsible person" for a school &mdash; typically the headteacher, governing body or trust, depending on how responsibility is delegated &mdash; must keep the fire risk assessment "under review". There's no fixed annual deadline written into the Order itself. Instead, the duty is ongoing: the assessment has to stay accurate and relevant to how the building is actually used.</p>
<h2>What "in practice" looks like</h2>
<p>In practice, most fire safety guidance and insurers recommend a full review at least annually, with an earlier review triggered by any of the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>A significant change to the building &mdash; a new extension, refurbishment, or change of use for a space</li>
<li>A change in how many people occupy the building, or a new activity (an after-school club, community lettings, holiday use)</li>
<li>Any fire, near-miss, or fire safety enforcement action</li>
<li>A change to escape routes, fire doors, alarm systems or other fire safety provisions</li>
<li>New information suggesting the current assessment is no longer suitable</li>
</ul>
<p>For most schools, that means: a full formal review once a year is sensible as a baseline, with a "living document" mindset the rest of the time &mdash; if something changes on site, the assessment should be revisited there and then, not left until the next scheduled review.</p>
<h2>Who's responsible for making sure it happens</h2>
<p>Responsibility can't simply be assumed to sit with the site manager by default. For maintained schools this typically sits with the headteacher or governing body; for academies, with the trust. Whoever holds it needs a clear, dated record of when the assessment was last reviewed, what changed, and who signed it off &mdash; this is exactly what an inspector or insurer will ask to see first.</p>
<h2>Getting it right</h2>
<p>A fire risk assessment that's technically "in date" but was clearly done as a box-ticking exercise won't hold up to scrutiny. The best assessments come from someone who actually walks the site, talks to staff about how spaces are used day-to-day, and checks that escape routes, signage and alarm testing records match what's happening in reality &mdash; not just what's on the floor plan.</p>
<p>If it's been more than a year since your last review, or your site has changed significantly since it was last done, <a href="/contact/">get in touch for a free 30-minute consultation</a> and we'll help you work out where you stand.</p>
<blockquote>This article is general guidance, not legal advice. For a definitive view on your specific site, consult a suitably qualified fire risk assessor or your local fire and rescue service.</blockquote>
</div>
