    <h1>Martyn's Law: What Schools, Academies and Community Venues in the North West Need to Do Before 2027</h1>
  </div>
</section>
<section>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="prose" style="max-width:720px">
<p>Martyn's Law &mdash; officially the Terrorism (Protection of Premises) Act 2025 &mdash; is coming into force for schools, sports clubs, community centres and other public-facing venues across the North West. The Act itself received Royal Assent and came into force on 3 April 2025, but the government has built in a 24-month implementation period before the duties become legally enforceable. That puts the real compliance deadline at spring 2027, with the Home Office publishing its final statutory guidance in April 2026.</p>
<p>If you run a school, academy, community centre or sports club, here's what you need to know now &mdash; not in six months' time.</p>
<h2>Does it apply to you?</h2>
<p>Martyn's Law applies if it's reasonable to expect <strong>200 or more people</strong> to be present on your premises at once &mdash; even if that only happens occasionally, such as at a school fete, sports day, prize-giving or evening event. If your site never realistically has more than 199 people on it at any one time, the law doesn't apply.</p>
<p>Education settings sit in the <strong>standard tier</strong> regardless of their capacity &mdash; the enhanced tier (which brings additional physical security requirements) doesn't apply to schools. That's good news: standard-tier duties are about documented procedures and staff training, not turnstiles and bag scanners.</p>
<h2>Who's responsible?</h2>
<p>For maintained schools, the <strong>responsible person</strong> is the governing body. For academies, it's the <strong>trust board</strong>. Either way, responsibility can't just be quietly delegated to a site manager and forgotten about &mdash; the guidance expects sign-off and ongoing oversight at governance level.</p>
<h2>What standard-tier compliance actually looks like</h2>
<p>For most schools and community venues, meeting the standard tier means:</p>
<ul>
<li>Having a documented plan for what staff should do in the event of an attack (invacuation, lockdown, evacuation)</li>
<li>Training relevant staff so they know that plan and their role in it</li>
<li>Reviewing and updating the plan when the site, its use, or its typical numbers change</li>
</ul>
<p>It's proportionate &mdash; this isn't about turning your school into a fortress. It's about having a clear, rehearsed procedure that staff actually know, rather than a policy document that sits in a drawer.</p>
<h2>What to do before spring 2027</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Work out your numbers.</strong> Look honestly at your busiest events, not just an average school day, to establish whether you cross the 200-person threshold.</li>
<li><strong>Identify your responsible person</strong> and get the topic onto a governors' or trustees' agenda now, not next term.</li>
<li><strong>Draft or review your procedures</strong> against the Home Office's April 2026 statutory guidance.</li>
<li><strong>Plan staff training</strong> &mdash; this needs to be genuine and refreshed, not a box-ticking exercise done once and forgotten.</li>
<li><strong>Build it into your existing compliance calendar</strong> alongside fire risk assessments and other statutory reviews, so it doesn't fall through the cracks.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Where this fits with everything else</h2>
<p>Martyn's Law is landing on top of an already busy compliance calendar for schools and community venues &mdash; fire risk assessments, health and safety inspections, safeguarding changes due from the Crime and Policing Act 2026 for the 2026/27 academic year, and routine statutory maintenance. Treating each of these as a one-off task rather than part of a joined-up compliance programme is where things start to slip.</p>
<p>If you'd like a second pair of eyes on where your site currently stands against the standard-tier requirements, or want it folded into a wider compliance audit, <a href="/contact/">get in touch for a free 30-minute consultation</a>.</p>
<blockquote>This article is general guidance, not legal advice. For a definitive view on your specific site, consult the Home Office's statutory guidance or a suitably qualified security adviser.</blockquote>
